        {
            "datasource": "Metrics",
            "description": "One row per Aurora MySQL cluster (AWS region, account and cluster ID), built from each instance's MySQL exporter.\n\n- **Cluster**: DB cluster identifier, read by rds_exporter from AWS when it starts. *AWS cluster unknown*: the instance has no rds_exporter (added with `pmm-admin add mysql`, or discovered on a pmm-agent older than PMM Server), so its cluster is not known. This row can lump instances of several clusters, so Writers there reads *changing*.\n- **Monitored instances**: Aurora MySQL services PMM monitors in the cluster (`mysql_up`), including those whose exporter stopped reporting within the last hour. Live.\n- **Exporters connected**: how many of the monitored instances have their MySQL exporter connected to the database (`mysql_up` = 1), out of Monitored instances. Live.\n- **Writers**: instances with `innodb_read_only` = 0. Sampled every minute, so it shows *changing* instead of *1 writer* during a failover or when no instance reports its role.\n- **Queries/s**: queries per second over the cluster's instances (MySQL `Queries` status counter). Live.\n- **Storage used**: cluster volume in use, from CloudWatch `VolumeBytesUsed` per cluster, counted once per cluster. AWS publishes it irregularly, so it can be up to about 3 hours old.",
